-- `try` is a block in which `?` returns from the **block**, lowered to an
-- immediately-invoked closure because Rust has try blocks only unstably —
-- examples/fallible.sdx makes the same point about why the closure is there.
--
-- So the claim worth asserting is not that the generated closure matches a
-- hand-written closure; it would, trivially. It is that the wrapper is
-- **free**: the hand-written half uses `?` in the function body directly,
-- with no closure at all, and the two must still optimise to the same IR.
--
-- Nothing here calls a helper, deliberately. The first version of this pair
-- had both halves call a `parse_it`, and it **failed** — rustc inlined that
-- helper into the hand-written half and not through the closure in the
-- generated one, so the IR differed by an inlining decision about a *third*
-- function rather than by anything the `try` lowering costs. `?` on a
-- parameter leaves the closure as the only variable.

attempt : Result<i32, String> -> Result<i32, String>
attempt r =
  try
    a = r?
    Ok (a + 1)
